# Plugin development

Work on `plugin/skills/@@PLUGIN_ID@@/SKILL.md` and its resources. Identity,
maintainers, capabilities and dependencies belong in `plugin/openstudio-si-plugin.yaml`.
The manifest version is the release version and the Python build reads it directly.
The authoring API range is independent of that version and the core version.
Add core as a dependency only for capabilities that use it. Skills and MCP
servers are composable contents, not plugin types.

Install developer tooling with `python -m pip install "openstudio-si[plugin-dev]"`.
It includes the modeling runtime and SDK. Use a published release that includes
plugin tools. This lab project publishes its plugin to the marketplace, not PyPI.

```bash
openstudio-si plugin check-project .
openstudio-si plugin validate ./plugin
openstudio-si plugin export ./plugin --output ./dist/plugin
openstudio-si plugin check-hosts ./dist/export-report.json
```

Save the export command's JSON output to dist/export-report.json when using
check-hosts. Before creating a release tag, run locally:

```bash
openstudio-si plugin check-project . --require-tracked
```

This local check detects present-but-untracked/ignored export inputs, including
nested assets/dist and assets/build. A fresh CI checkout cannot see omitted
ignored files; its same check is only a consistency check, not evidence that no
files were lost before checkout. Review the local file set before tagging.
Host manifests/descriptors/launchers are generated from tracked source inputs.

CI initially has manual configuration stubs only. Configure or upgrade thin
reusable-workflow calls with plugin configure-ci using an immutable PNNL workflow
SHA and an exact published openstudio-si version. Shared logic belongs in the installed
wheel. Do not copy its host loader or release-record code into this repository.

Use deterministic domain checks for engineering operations. Declare dependencies
for each capability. Metadata validation does not prove runtime readiness, and
cached files do not establish host enablement. Cases and reviewed references
live under plugin/evals. Agent/judge execution is developer-run work using openstudio-si eval run. Judge scores are advisory. Never add LLM calls to CI.

Keep credentials out of source, arguments, logs and artifacts. MCP environment
settings reference variables. Respect the explicitly chosen license and copyright
holder; obtain the contributor organization's legal/IP approval before release.

For release, bump the manifest version, review dependencies and publish a matching
GitHub tag v<manifest-version>. The configured read-only build creates candidate
archives. A separate upload-only job stages them on that source release for the
planned central importer. The intended publication is a plugin-scoped PR to
pnnl/openstudio-si-plugins. Automatic submission is step E and remains pending;
archive upload alone does not mean the marketplace has published the plugin.

The SDK is installed with `openstudio-si[plugin-dev]`, but generation and
packaging do not import it or require a native OpenStudio CLI. Budget roughly
370 MB for the environment; size varies with platform and dependencies. The
pinned OpenStudio 3.11.0 package currently has no Windows Python 3.14 wheel;
use a supported Python/platform combination (CI uses Linux/Python 3.13).


## Local agent evaluation with DeepEval

Install `python -m pip install "openstudio-si[eval]"`. Edit the generated
`eval-run.example.yaml`, select agent/judge models and the DeepEval judge supplier, set credential
ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES, and save as `eval-run.local.yaml`. Export first, then run:

```bash
openstudio-si eval run --plugin ./plugin --cases ./plugin/evals/cases \
  --config ./eval-run.local.yaml --output ./outputs/evals/run-001
openstudio-si eval report --run ./outputs/evals/run-001
```

Plugin paths are relative to the config file. Cases and run/result contracts are
draft 0.1. The first host drivers use API/gateway credentials in isolated state,
not existing subscription login files. Claude needs explicit allowed_tools for
shell/edit tasks. Codex currently cannot verify skill activation through exec
JSONL; those checks are marked not_observable with notes. Codex defaults to
read-only; opt into agent.sandbox: workspace-write for edits. Host capability
profiles are recorded, and MCP policies remain plugin-defined. Text model checks do not validate
sizing; metrics quoted in prose are unverified notes. SQL/binary content
extraction is not implemented; a metric requiring file contents must declare
required_artifacts and include context. Keep reviewed references out of inputs. Cases configure G-Eval criteria or steps, evidence parameters and optional score bands. Judge scores stay advisory. Keep all actual LLM execution out of CI;
unit-testing the framework with local stubs requires no model credentials.

Hosted judges receive selected evaluation data, potentially including full tool
traces, reference text and OSM/project artifacts. Known-secret redaction is not
project-data anonymization. Use Ollama or an approved internal gateway for sensitive
data. Proxy/CA environment settings are inherited; MCP-declared variables are shared
with the agent process and listed in the capability metadata.

Re-export after every source or case edit. Use expected.model.path to select a
saved output when a workflow generates several OSM files; server intermediates are
kept as evidence. The report labels SI's weighted score separately from native
DeepEval per-metric pass. Both are advisory.

The optional plugin/evals/calibration/judge-injection-canary.json exercises a judge
with a deliberately wrong answer containing scoring instructions. Run eval with
--cases ./plugin/evals/calibration in a separate output directory and inspect the
actual answer. A low correctness score is intended. JSON evidence boundaries and
grading rules reduce injection exposure, but each real provider/model needs local
calibration. This canary is not part of the default cases or CI.
